## 3. Scientific Conclusions & Interpretation

- **Primary Result (Joint vs. Fixed RIS)**: At 10,000 training slots, joint-minus-fixed utility difference is $+0.0183$ (95% CI $[-0.1677, +0.2043]$, $p=0.8289$). Fixed and joint secrecy metric means are 1.5656 and 1.4839 bps/Hz ($p=0.4182$). Neither comparison provides evidence of joint-control superiority; fixed alignment toward Bob achieves equivalent or numerically higher secrecy while exploring a smaller action space (625 vs. 3,125 actions).
- **Multiplicity Sensitivity**: Secondary contrasts are sensitive to full-family 18-hypothesis Holm adjustments: fixed secrecy Holm-$t$ $p=0.0696$, joint utility Holm-$t$ $p=0.3166$, and fixed utility Holm sign-flip $p=0.0703$. Blanket claims of significant gains across both metrics are not justified.
- **Zero-Shot Geometry Transfer**: Discrete geometry-aware oracles maintain positive utility ($0.7119$--$1.0418$), but nominal-trained policies deployed zero-shot without retraining suffer negative utility ($-0.5608$ to $-0.7876$) and large optimality gaps ($>1.6$). In swapped positions, frozen policies suffer secrecy collapse ($R_s=0.0$), whereas the oracle directs reflections toward Eve ($q=2$, utility $+0.7719$). In nearby-Eve and colocated geometries, physical-layer secrecy is identically 0.0000.
- **Physical 4-PAM Channel Feasibility**: Positive secrecy differences (1.65–1.73 bits/channel use) are QoS-feasible ($P_{b,B} \le 10^{-3}$) only at 20–30 dB reference SNR. At 10 dB SNR, no action meets the QoS constraint. In the colocated setting, secrecy is identically 0.000000 bits/use across all SNRs.

